    "risks": [
      "Managers are the least enthusiastic group, and if they quietly treat home days as less committed, staff will doubt the promise that reviews and promotion are unchanged, eroding much of the retention gain.",
      "Several long-commute voices say they would still be looking around; the policy eases a pain point but does not fix the underlying job, so the effect may be smaller or fade if other issues remain.",
      "A fixed Wednesday and Friday pattern may not suit every team's workload, and a home Friday can be read as an informal long weekend, inviting pushback from leaders and pressure to roll the policy back. Reversing a popular benefit would likely do more damage than never offering it."
